  },
  "articleBody": "It's easy to get interior stain and sealer for log homes mixed up, but they actually serve slightly different purposes, though they often work together. Think of stain primarily as a way to add color and enhance the natural beauty of your logs. Interior stains for log homes are commonly used and often preferred over paint because they allow the wood to breathe. They penetrate the wood fibers, changing or enriching the wood's tone while still letting the natural grain show through. A sealer, on the other hand, is generally a clear topcoat designed to protect the stained or natural wood surface. It acts as a barrier against moisture, dust, and wear, making the logs easier to clean and helping to prevent deterioration. You can definitely use a clear coat on interior log surfaces, and many log home owners choose a stain-plus-clear system to get both color and maximum protection. The key is choosing products specifically made for log homes because they're formulated to handle the unique characteristics and movement of log surfaces. Generic wood finishes might not provide the same breathable protection. If you're unsure whether your logs need just a clear coat, a stain, or a full system, HH Remodeling LLC can help you sort out the best approach for your home.",
